Proxmox login lifecycle and response validation
==============================================

Malformed ticket envelopes, unexpected transport errors and cancelled login
attempts now close the HTTP pool allocated by connect(). A failed async context
entry does not rely on __aexit__, which Python does not call for failed entry.
Only an authenticated open connection satisfies repeated connect(). A stranded
unauthenticated pool is closed before starting a fresh login.

Password login requires an object envelope containing an object data field and a
nonempty string ticket. Ticket and optional string CSRF fields cannot contain CR,
LF or NUL. Missing/empty CSRF remains compatible. Malformed responses raise a fixed
authentication error without the response body. API-token authentication remains
separate, and a rejected token never falls back to a password.

close() clears authentication, local ticket/CSRF state and the client cache identity
in a finally block, including when transport closure fails. During failed login,
a secondary closure error does not replace the original error or cancellation;
its log uses a fixed message. Existing transport failure/cooldown classification
and valid shared-ticket reuse remain unchanged.

Limits: this does not implement concurrent shared-client ownership, distributed
operation fencing, or server-side ticket revocation. It cannot guarantee that a
broken transport or repeated cancellation completes resource cleanup; cleared
local state prevents reuse but is not proof of successful remote closure. A
synthetic TLS peer does not establish live Proxmox hardware acceptance. Update
all workers and API instances; process-local caches are not migrated across
old/new running versions.
